---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import MosaicMark from "../components/MosaicMark.astro";
import ArtworkCatalogue from "../components/ArtworkCatalogue.astro";
import ThemedArtwork from "@greenways-ai/visual-language/ThemedArtwork.astro";

const title = "Hestia · Personal keystore and signed Hara state chain";
const description = "A local-first personal keystore and append-only state chain for human and agent keys, with state expressed and replayed as Hara/HAL AST.";
const base = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, "")}/`;
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <ThemedArtwork class="hero-art" light={`${base}assets/hestia-greyhound-day.webp`} dark={`${base}assets/hestia-greyhound-night.webp`} lightMobile={`${base}assets/hestia-greyhound-day.webp`} darkMobile={`${base}assets/hestia-greyhound-night.webp`} lightAlt="A greyhound standing beside a garnet couch in a couture winter garden" darkAlt="The greyhound asleep on the garnet couch beside a lit hearth" position="center" />
    <div class="hero-veil"></div>
    <div class="hero-copy section-shell">
      <p class="technical-kicker"><i></i> Personal keystore · signed Hara state</p>
      <h1 id="hero-title">Your keys.<br />Your state.<br />Your chain.</h1>
      <p>Hestia keeps human and agent keys under your control and records every accepted transition as content-addressed, replayable Hara state. Run it locally, inspect its history, and add recovery or collaboration without surrendering custody.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="action action--ember" href="#operate">Run Hestia locally <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
        <a class="action" href="#state-chain">Understand the state chain <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
        <a class="action" href="https://github.com/greenways-ai/hestia">View source ↗</a>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-command"><code>scripts/hestia up</code><span>Personal node · open source</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-inscription" aria-hidden="true"><span>OWN</span><i></i><span>SIGN</span><i></i><span>REPLAY</span><i></i><span>EXTEND</span></div>
  </section>

  <section id="overview" class="home-section purpose-section" aria-labelledby="overview-title">
    <header class="section-heading">
      <p class="technical-kicker">01 · Personal keystore</p>
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Authority before accounts</p><h2 id="overview-title">Keep authority<br />close to home.</h2></div>
      <p>Hestia begins with keys you control. Human identities, agent profiles and delegated operational keys remain anchored to a personal root rather than to a service account.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="purpose-grid">
      <article><b>01</b><h3>Root identities</h3><p>Create and rotate the keys that represent a person or long-lived agent profile without making a hosted platform the source of identity.</p></article>
      <article><b>02</b><h3>Delegated keys</h3><p>Give an agent only the purposes it needs. Operational and room keys can be scoped, replaced and revoked without replacing the root.</p></article>
      <article><b>03</b><h3>Local custody</h3><p>Browser and node runtimes keep private key material close to the operator. Export, backup and publication remain explicit choices.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="state-chain" class="home-section purpose-section" aria-labelledby="state-chain-title">
    <header class="section-heading">
      <p class="technical-kicker">02 · Signed state chain</p>
      <div><p class="eyebrow">A personal blockchain without a token network</p><h2 id="state-chain-title">State with<br />a memory.</h2></div>
      <p>Hestia is an append-only chain of accepted state roots. Each transition can be signed, linked to the state before it, replayed through the Hara kernel and verified independently.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="purpose-grid">
      <article><b>01</b><h3>Hara semantics</h3><p>State, policy and transition intent are represented as Hara/HAL values and AST rather than hidden inside one database implementation.</p></article>
      <article><b>02</b><h3>Signed roots</h3><p>Canonical HCV1 cells produce content-addressed roots that can be linked, signed and carried between browser and server runtimes.</p></article>
      <article><b>03</b><h3>Replayable history</h3><p>Receipts and previous roots make accepted change inspectable. Adapters may change while the semantic chain remains portable.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="trust-section" aria-labelledby="chain-proof-title">
    <div class="trust-art">
      <picture>
        <source srcset={`${base}assets/hestia-local-ledger.webp`} type="image/webp" />
        <img src={`${base}assets/hestia-local-ledger.jpg`} width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" alt="An ember-lit mosaic hearth connected to concentric ledger nodes" />
      </picture>
      <MosaicMark class="trust-mark" label="Hestia signed state chain" />
    </div>
    <div class="trust-copy">
      <p class="technical-kicker"><i></i> 03 · Semantic state, durable evidence</p>
      <h2 id="chain-proof-title">The meaning lives<br />above the database.</h2>
      <p>Hara owns the deterministic state model. HCV1 supplies canonical content roots. IndexedDB and PostgreSQL provide storage and multi-writer operation without becoming the definition of the state itself.</p>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Semantic state</dt><dd>Hara/HAL AST, values, policies and transitions</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Durable encoding</dt><dd>Canonical HCV1 cells, roots, signatures and receipts</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Storage adapters</dt><dd>Browser IndexedDB or a PostgreSQL-backed personal node</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <a class="action action--ember" href="https://github.com/greenways-ai/hestia/blob/main/docs/document-protocol-v1.md">Read a state protocol ↗</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="architecture" class="home-section architecture-section" aria-labelledby="architecture-title">
    <header class="section-heading">
      <p class="technical-kicker">04 · Core architecture</p>
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Every authority boundary made visible</p><h2 id="architecture-title">One core.<br />Replaceable edges.</h2></div>
      <p>Keys establish authority, Hara describes state and policy, the signed chain records accepted roots, and adapters provide storage, transport and cryptography as explicit capabilities.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="architecture-flow" aria-label="Hestia architecture flows from user and agent keys through Hara state into a signed HCV1 chain and replaceable storage adapters">
      <span><small>OWN</small><b>User + agent keys</b><em>authority</em></span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
      <span><small>DESCRIBE</small><b>Hara / HAL AST</b><em>state</em></span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
      <span class="architecture-core"><small>COMMIT</small><b>HCV1 chain</b><em>signed roots</em></span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
      <span><small>ADAPT</small><b>Browser + Postgres</b><em>storage</em></span>
    </div>
    <div class="capability-grid">
      <article><span>01</span><strong>Semantic core</strong><p>Deterministic Hara kernels decide state transitions before storage or transport adapters act.</p></article>
      <article><span>02</span><strong>Content-addressed chain</strong><p>Canonical roots connect state versions and make exact accepted values independently verifiable.</p></article>
      <article><span>03</span><strong>Explicit capabilities</strong><p>Cryptography, persistence and communication are requested at visible boundaries instead of hidden inside policy code.</p></article>
      <article><span>04</span><strong>Replaceable infrastructure</strong><p>Run locally in a browser, operate a PostgreSQL node, or replace a relay without replacing personal authority.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="extensions" class="home-section purpose-section" aria-labelledby="extensions-title">
    <header class="section-heading">
      <p class="technical-kicker">05 · Optional extensions</p>
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Built on Hestia, not confused with Hestia</p><h2 id="extensions-title">Keys and state<br />come first.</h2></div>
      <p>Documents, recovery ceremonies and private agent rooms use the keystore and signed state chain. They are useful Hestia extensions, but none of them defines the core product.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="purpose-grid">
      <article><b>01</b><h3>Documents</h3><p>Sign document versions, provenance and operation roots while keeping the canonical state portable and replayable.</p></article>
      <article><b>02</b><h3>Recovery</h3><p>Coordinate independent approvals and threshold reconstruction without introducing a Greenways master key or plaintext share custodian.</p></article>
      <article><b>03</b><h3>Agent Rooms</h3><p>Use profiles, delegated keys and exact roots for bounded membership, encrypted communication and human-approved negotiation.</p></article>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="action action--ember" href={`${base}recovery-demo/`}>Open Recovery extension ↗</a>
      <a class="action" href={`${base}rooms/`}>Open Agent Rooms extension ↗</a>
      <a class="action" href="https://github.com/greenways-ai/hestia/tree/main/docs">Browse protocols ↗</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="services" class="trust-section" aria-labelledby="services-title">
    <div class="trust-art">
      <picture>
        <source srcset={`${base}assets/hestia-recovery-keepers.webp`} type="image/webp" />
        <img src={`${base}assets/hestia-recovery-keepers.jpg`} width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" alt="Three independent mosaic sanctuary chambers joined around a central circle" />
      </picture>
      <MosaicMark class="trust-mark" label="Greenways services powered by Hestia" />
    </div>
    <div class="trust-copy">
      <p class="technical-kicker"><i></i> 06 · Greenways service layer</p>
      <h2 id="services-title">Services can help.<br />They do not own.</h2>
      <p>Greenways can operate ceremony coordination, availability, invitation delivery and blind relay services around Hestia. The personal node remains the root of identity, membership and accepted state.</p>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Recovery service</dt><dd>Coordinates accredited keepers, notifications and policy workflows</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Rooms service</dt><dd>Provides rendezvous, relay, presence and optional encrypted mailbox delivery</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Trust boundary</dt><dd>No service master key; final authority remains with participant keys and Hestia roots</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="action action--ember" href={`${base}recovery-demo/`}>Try Recovery service preview ↗</a>
        <a class="action" href={`${base}rooms/`}>Try Rooms service preview ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="operate" class="operate-section" aria-labelledby="operate-title">
    <picture class="operate-art">
      <source srcset={`${base}assets/hestia-local-ledger.webp`} type="image/webp" />
      <img src={`${base}assets/hestia-local-ledger.jpg`} width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" alt="An ember-lit mosaic hearth connected to concentric ledger nodes" />
    </picture>
    <div class="operate-veil"></div>
    <div class="operate-copy section-shell">
      <p class="technical-kicker"><i></i> 07 · Run your personal node</p>
      <h2 id="operate-title">Keep the hearth<br />close to home.</h2>
      <p>One operator command starts the deliberately small Hestia stack. The node owns its local secrets, validates signed state and exposes extensions only when the operator chooses.</p>
      <pre><code>scripts/hestia init
scripts/hestia doctor
scripts/hestia up</code></pre>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Core</dt><dd>Personal keystore + signed Hara state chain</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Distribution</dt><dd>Browser runtime and source-built local node</dd></div>
        <div><dt>License</dt><dd>Apache-2.0</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="action action--ember" href="https://github.com/greenways-ai/hestia">View source ↗</a>
        <a class="action" href="https://github.com/greenways-ai/hestia/blob/main/docs/local-node.md">Read the operator guide ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <ArtworkCatalogue />
</BaseLayout>
